ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: yellow
PROMPT_VERSION: v001
EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [
  {system: "mcp-tool-search", url: "https://github.com/KGT24k/mcp-tool-search", overlap: "Shipping MCP proxy snapshots all backend tool definitions into a catalog and searches flat over tool.name + tool.description + serverName; this implements the packet's flat/no-ceiling mitigation, not editorial server-aggregate routing."},
  {system: "Microsoft MCP routing tutorial", url: "https://github.com/microsoft/mcp-for-beginners/blob/main/05-AdvancedTopics/mcp-routing/README.md", overlap: "Shows content/category-based routing to MCP services/servers before service use; overlaps with server-first routing, but no tool-level recall ceiling or coverage-gap analysis."},
  {system: "OpenViking", url: "https://github.com/volcengine/OpenViking", overlap: "Uses hierarchical/directory recursive retrieval: locate a high-score directory then refine within it. Mechanistically adjacent to aggregate-then-member retrieval, but it is RAG context retrieval, not MCP tool routing."},
  {system: "vLLM", url: "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/blob/main/docs/features/tool_calling.md", overlap: "Supports tool calling/parsing with caller-supplied tools; no evidence of OSS server-to-tool retrieval or editorial aggregate routing."},
  {system: "LMCache / FlashInfer / TensorRT-LLM / SGLang", url: "https://github.com/LMCache/LMCache ; https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer ; https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM ; https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang", overlap: "Primarily serving/cache/kernel/tool-call-parser infrastructure; no evidence found that they implement this MCP-style two-stage tool retrieval mechanism."}
]
CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: mechanism
SCALE_REALISM: L0 supports the synthetic mechanism cleanly: flat=1.000, two-stage gap +0.467 +/- 0.013 at S=40,c=0.5,m=1, 39,312/39,312 misses attributed to absent distinctive tokens, and controls c=1.0 or m=S close the gap. But realistic-scale evidence is insufficient: S<=40,T=8 is small for MCP-scale registries; stronger stage-1 is lexical only; no dense encoder/router; no measurements on real MCP/ToolBench registries; and at least one shipping MCP search proxy pools member tool descriptions directly, making the editorial-undercoverage premise non-universal.
RATIONALE: Yellow, not red, because the packet demonstrates a real reachability mechanism rather than mere integration glue: if stage-1 only sees a lossy server description and top-m<S, within-server reranking cannot recover an un-routed server. Yellow, not green, because this is close to established hierarchical retrieval/collection-selection/IVF failure logic, and the packet has not shown that shipping agent systems commonly route on short editorial aggregates rather than pooled tool schemas. The OSS sweep cuts against broad ecological validity: mcp-tool-search explicitly pre-scans all MCP servers and builds a flat per-tool catalog, while vLLM-style systems leave tool selection to caller-supplied tool lists rather than implementing this router. Required L1: run real MCP/ToolBench registry as-written descriptions, compare editorial-server vs pooled-member vs flat retrieval, include dense embeddings/reranker, and report recall/latency under realistic registry sizes.
